--TEST--
`Core\Taint::assertTrusted` answers its operand byte for byte, takes a reason that is not required to be a literal, and is legal on a value that was never tainted — ADR 0024 § 3
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 3 asks for a written reason and for nothing to be done to the value: the
// whole member is a compile-time judgement, so the run-time answer has to be
// the argument itself. The values below are the ones a member that trimmed,
// escaped or re-encoded anything would answer plausibly on and fail here — an
// empty string, the characters every escaper reaches for, a value that is only
// padding, and one whose bytes are not one code unit each.
tainted string $empty = "" as tainted string;
tainted string $markup = "<a href='x'>Tom & Jerry</a>" as tainted string;
tainted string $padded = "  spaced  " as tainted string;
tainted string $wide = "ünïcodé" as tainted string;

echo "[", Core\Taint::assertTrusted($empty, "the case wrote this value itself"), "]\n";
echo "[", Core\Taint::assertTrusted($markup, "the case wrote this value itself"), "]\n";
echo "[", Core\Taint::assertTrusted($padded, "the case wrote this value itself"), "]\n";
echo "[", Core\Taint::assertTrusted($wide, "the case wrote this value itself"), "]\n";

// The reason is checked as an ordinary `string` and read by nobody. § 3 asks
// for it to be written, not for it to be a literal — a binding holding one is
// as greppable at the call site as the text would have been, which is
// `nvs_stdlib::secret`'s reason for the same omission one axis over.
string $why = "the value was matched against an allowlist at the request boundary";
echo Core\Taint::assertTrusted($markup, $why), "\n";

// And asserting a value that was never tainted is the identity and is legal.
// Refusing it would cost a diagnostic and prevent no injection: the qualifier
// is a bit on the type, so a member that insisted on seeing it set would refuse
// the very code that already did the right thing.
string $plain = "plain";
echo Core\Taint::assertTrusted($plain, "nothing to assert, and that is legal"), "\n";
--EXPECT--
[]
[<a href='x'>Tom & Jerry</a>]
[  spaced  ]
[ünïcodé]
<a href='x'>Tom & Jerry</a>
plain
